Management, Technical, and Operational Controls



controls can be classified into three main types: management, technical, and operational. additionally, a common control is a subset of these main control types.


each type of control has a different purpose, scope, and responsibility in maintaining a secure and compliant environment. these controls are further defined in the following sections. 


Management Controls: management controls are the administrative and organizational controls established by senior management to shape the security culture and governance structure within an organization and provide strategic direction, oversight, and accountability for security compliance. examples of management controls include the security and compliance policies that define goals, objectives, roles, and responsibilities of the organization. the risk management process that identities, assess, and prioritizes, and mitigates the risks to the organizations information assets. the security and compliance audits that evaluate the effectiveness and efficiency of the controls and identify any gaps of weaknesses. the security and compliance training awareness programs that educate the staff and stakeholders on the policies, procedures, standards, and guidelines of the organization

management controls are essential for establishing the security and compliance culture and governance of the organization. they provide high level vision, direction, and support for the implementation and maintenance of the other types of controls. through these controls, senior management can communicate the importance of information security and compliance, setting the tone for culture that values and protects information assets. management controls enable the organization to systematically assess and adjust its security strategies in response to ongoing monitoring and review processes, ensuring the security posture remains robust and proactive in addressing new challenges and threats. 



Technical Controls: technical controls are the technological controls (e.g. hardware, software, etc.) implemented to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of information assets. they provide the technical means and mechanisms for enforcing the security and compliance policies and standards of the organization. examples of technical controls include: the encryption and decryption of data in transit and at rest. the authentication and authorization of users and devices. the firewall and antivirus software that prevent and detect unauthorized access and malicious attacks. the backup and recovery systems that ensure the availability and resilience of data. 

technical controls are crucial for delivering the security and compliance capabilities of information systems. they offer foundational protection, detection, and response mechanisms for information assets. these controls facilitate the safeguarding of data systems at a granular level, ensuring the confidentiality, integrity and availability of organizational resources. by implementing technical controls, organizations can enforce their security policies and compliance requirements directly through their IT infrastructure, providing a robust defense against threats and vulnerabilities



Operational Controls: operational controls are specific to a particular information system or domain within an organization. they provide customized security and compliance requirements and standards that are tailored to the unique characteristics and needs of the information systems and include procedures and measures that help maintain the secure functioning of these systems. by focusing on the specific requirements and characteristics of each information asset or system, operational controls ensure security measures are appropriately applied and managed. examples of operational controls include: granting and revoking permissions and privileges to the information asset. labeling and handling the information asset according to its sensitivity and criticality. data retention and disposal of the information asset according to its life cycle and legal obligations. data quality, integrity, and completeness of the information asset. 

operational controls are essential for providing the security and compliance specificity and effectives of the information asset. they provide fin grained and tailored protection, detection, and response to information assets. 



Common Controls: common controls are shared by multiple information systems or domains within an organization. these controls may fall under the category of management, technical, or operational. they are inherited from other systems or are third party vendor controls that can be implemented. they provide the baseline security and compliance requirements and standard applicable to all or most of the information assets. the use of common controls allows for a more integrated and coherent security strategy, facilitating easier management, monitoring, and reporting of security and compliance across the organization. examples of common control include: enterprise wide security policies, governance frameworks, and risk assessment methodologies. centralized authentication mechanisms and network security measures. the physical security of the premises and facilities house the information assets. the network security of the infrastructure and devices that connect the information assts. the configuration management of the hardware and software components of the information assets. incident management of the reporting and handling of security and compliance events and incidents. 

common controls are essential for providing security and compliance, consistency, and efficiency throughout the organization. they provide centralized, cross cutting, and shared protection, detection, and response for the information assets.


controls are the key elements of security and compliance in an organization. by understanding and applying the different types of controls, the GRC professional can achieve a comprehensive and balanced security and compliance posture. this holistic approach to security and compliance, integrating various types of controls, ensures that an organization can protect its assets, data, and systems from multiple angles, thereby achieving a robust, comprehensive, and balanced security and compliance posture. this integrated security framework not only mitigates risks but also enhances the organizations ability to respond to and recover from security incidents, ensuring business continuity and the protection of critical assets.



operational controls are the most specific and effective for protecting information assets because they define the handling, access, and usage of these assets.



